{"candidate_actions":[
    {"action_id":"sha256:250e98df4fdae10cf876d87573b6c87542936c9575db60e607291fb6dc11e69e","violated_requirement":"After detecting a mismatch in sf4 state, the state machine should progress to s1 to start checking the next group immediately rather than continuing through sf5","spec_basis":"The specification requires processing groups of six bits without overlapping. When a mismatch is detected, the next group should start immediately at the next bit."},
    {"action_id":"sha256:63969fb9c80e9f5d6f5df8ce4b62ffaa714135fd3d047211beaee1229bcadc34","violated_requirement":"In sf5 state, transitioning to s1 would allow immediate checking of the next group after a mismatch is confirmed, rather than waiting through sf6","spec_basis":"The sequence check must begin with every sixth bit of input data, so after confirming a mismatch in sf5, the state machine should reset to check the next group."}
]}

{
  "edits": [
    {
      "file": "rtl/sequence.sv",
      "find": "sf4:\n            nstate=data?sf5:s1;",
      "replace": "sf4:\n            nstate=sf5;"
    }
  ],
  "edit": "The failure occurs when processing a group starting with 000000. The state machine incorrectly transitions from sf4 to s1 when data is 0, causing incorrect classification of the next group's first bit. The change makes sf4 always transition to sf5, which maintains proper group boundary detection. This should fix the mismatch observed at cycle 12 where the output was 00 but should have been 01 for the first bit of a new group."
}

{"edits": [{
    "file": "rtl/sequence.sv",
    "find": "sf6:\n\t\t\tnstate=data?sf1:s1;",
    "replace": "sf6:\n\t\t\tnstate=idle;"
}],
"edit": "The failure occurs at cycle 11 when the module is in state sf6 and outputs 01 (match=0, not_match=1) when it should output 00. The issue is in the sf6 state transition logic where it incorrectly transitions to sf1 or s1 based on data input. According to the specification, after completing a group (6 bits), the module should reset to idle state to begin checking the next group. Changing the sf6 transition to always go to idle fixes this by properly resetting the state machine after each group is processed."}

{
  "edits": [
    {
      "file": "rtl/sequence.sv",
      "find": "sf6:\n\t\t\tnstate=data?sf1:s1;",
      "replace": "sf6:\n\t\t\tnstate=idle;"
    }
  ],
  "edit": "The failure occurs at cycle 11 when the module is in state sf6 and incorrectly outputs match=0, not_match=1 (01) when it should output 00. The issue is in the sf6 state transition logic where it incorrectly transitions based on data input. According to the specification, after completing a group (6 bits), the module should reset to idle state to begin checking the next group. Changing the sf6 transition to always go to idle fixes this issue."
}

{
  "edits": [
    {
      "file": "rtl/sequence.sv",
      "find": "sf6:\n\t\t\tnstate=data?sf1:s1;",
      "replace": "sf6:\n\t\t\tnstate=sf1;"
    }
  ],
  "edit": "The failure occurs at cycle 11 when the state is sf6, where the outputs incorrectly show match=0 and not_match=1 when they should both be 00. The issue is in the state transition logic for sf6, which currently checks data to decide between sf1 and s1. This causes incorrect behavior when processing new groups. The fix removes the data-dependent transition, always moving to sf1 after sf6, which properly resets the sequence detection for the next group."
}

{
  "edits": [
    {
      "file": "rtl/sequence.sv",
      "find": "sf6:\n\t\t\tnstate=sf1;",
      "replace": "sf6:\n\t\t\tnstate=idle;"
    }
  ],
  "edit": "The failure occurs at cycle 11 when the state is sf6, where the outputs incorrectly show match=0 and not_match=1 when they should both be 00. The issue is in the state transition from sf6 - it should transition back to idle to start a new group detection, rather than going to sf1 which causes incorrect behavior for the next group. This change ensures proper reset of the state machine after completing a group check."
}
